<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!--
  Template for config.xml, the database settings the application reads on startup.

  config.xml is not tracked in git because it holds the credentials of whichever
  database a given machine talks to. Copy this file to config.xml and fill it in.

  ConnectionToDatabase resolves the plain name "config.xml" against the JVM
  working directory, so the file belongs next to whatever you launch: the repo
  root when running from the root, account/ when running that module directly.

  Every value below is encrypted and Base64-encoded, not plain text - the loader
  decrypts each element before use, so plain values fail at startup. Values
  written now begin with "v2:" and are AES/GCM, with a random IV each and an
  authentication tag, so an edited file is rejected instead of yielding altered
  settings. Values without that prefix are the older unauthenticated format and
  are still read, so existing installs keep starting.

  First set a key of this install's own. Writing a config.xml requires one: the
  built-in fallback key is published in the source, so anything encrypted with it
  can be read by anyone holding the repository.

    java -cp controlsfx/target/classes \
      com.hamza.controlsfx.util.crypto.CryptoDatabaseConfig genkey

  Put the printed value in the ACCOUNT_CONFIG_KEY environment variable, or in a
  file named config.key. config.key and config.xml are both resolved against the
  JVM working directory, so run the CLI from wherever the application launches.
  Keep the key out of version control and back it up - without it config.xml
  cannot be read.

  Then write the file:

    java -cp controlsfx/target/classes \
      com.hamza.controlsfx.util.crypto.CryptoDatabaseConfig \
      encrypt <port> <host> <dbname> <username> <password> config.xml

  To convert a config.xml that predates all this, install a new key as above and
  run "migrate config.xml". It reads the file under whichever key it was written
  with and rewrites it under the new one. Any credentials that were protected
  only by the built-in key should be changed on the database server as well:
  re-encrypting the file does not un-publish what the old file exposed.

  Other commands: "decrypt config.xml" prints the settings back.

  url is "jdbc:mysql://<host>:<port>/<dbname>" and driver is
  "com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver" before encryption.
-->
<DatabaseConfig>
    <url>ENCRYPTED_JDBC_URL</url>
    <dbname>ENCRYPTED_DATABASE_NAME</dbname>
    <host>ENCRYPTED_HOST</host>
    <username>ENCRYPTED_USERNAME</username>
    <password>ENCRYPTED_PASSWORD</password>
    <port>ENCRYPTED_PORT</port>
    <driver>ENCRYPTED_DRIVER_CLASS</driver>
</DatabaseConfig>
